---
// The appendix: backup slides for questions, reachable only by `a`, a slide number or the index. Each is a question,
// three to five bullets from the thesis, and where in the thesis it is. No names of jury members anywhere here.
import Slide from './Slide.astro';
import clamp from '../../data/defence/clamp.json';
const cs = clamp.summary;
const qs: { id: string; q: string; bullets: string[]; ref: string }[] = [
  { id: 'q1', q: 'What single question unifies the thesis?', ref: 'Ch. 1 §1.3, Ch. 11', bullets: [
    'How much does a simple local rule tell you about the global behaviour it produces, and what does the behaviour tell you about the rule and the network: genotype against phenotype.',
    'Three objectives: rigorous foundations for these systems; how network topology shapes the dynamics; the systems turned into instruments, for neural networks and for network science.',
    'In the thesis’s words: to map, understand and apply the link between iterating simple local rules and the rich landscape of emergent outcomes.',
    'Chapters 4 to 10 each answer one part; Chapter 11 puts the answers side by side.' ] },
  { id: 'q2', q: 'What makes the best classification rule special?', ref: 'Ch. 9 §9.6.3, §9.7', bullets: [
    'The analysis works at the level of partitions of the rule space by jaggedness, not rule by rule.',
    'The best individual rules were not examined one by one.',
    'Calling the rules in the sweet spot “complex” is a hypothesis; it was not tested.' ] },
  { id: 'q3', q: 'Is dynamical importance confounded by conditioning on consensus?', ref: 'Ch. 10 §10.2.2, §10.4', bullets: [
    'η pools clampings that change a node’s start (its hand was up) with ones that only confirm it (already down).',
    'Which starts reach consensus is itself linked to centrality; the thesis does not separate the two.',
    `On the toy network of tonight: Spearman(η, degree) = ${cs.spearmanEtaDegree.toFixed(2)} overall, ${cs.spearmanGenuineDegree.toFixed(2)} from the changing clampings only, ${cs.spearmanConfirmatoryDegree.toFixed(2)} from the confirming ones only.`,
    'So the ranking survives the split here, on one toy network; that is an illustration, not a result.' ] },
  { id: 'q4', q: 'Is your Lyapunov exponent really an entropy?', ref: 'Ch. 4', bullets: [
    'It is a tangent-space quantity: the growth rate of a count of walks along which a defect can travel, which makes it entropy-like by construction.',
    'For the parity rule on a graph the exponents are ln|λₖ(A) + a∘|, the maximal one ln ρ(A + a∘I); for the rule without self, the logarithms of the adjacency eigenvalues.',
    'The thesis itself calls it closer to an entropy than to a rate of separation.',
    'Wolfram (1984) introduced both notions for cellular automata side by side.' ] },
  { id: 'q5', q: 'Does small-world structure cause synchronisation?', ref: 'Ch. 6 §6.1.3', bullets: [
    'No causal claim. The rule fails for p ≤ 0.04, peaks near p ≈ 0.3 (94%), beyond the small-world band, and drops to 39% at p = 1.',
    'Short paths look necessary but not sufficient, for this rule.',
    'Rewired grids behave differently: 82% unrewired, above 95% for 0.02 ≤ p ≤ 0.4.',
    'At p = 0.2 the thesis reports 83% from the screening and 78% from the sweep, both at half the nodes on: two estimates of one point.' ] },
  { id: 'q6', q: 'Can a classifier discover behaviour classes, or only transfer known ones?', ref: 'Ch. 8', bullets: [
    'Its output only transfers the labels it was trained on.',
    'Its internal representation can support exploration without labels, but that is not discovery.',
    'A self-supervised attempt turned out hard.' ] },
  { id: 'q7', q: 'What do cellular automata offer interpretability research?', ref: 'Chs. 7, 8', bullets: [
    'Circuits whose weights are known exactly: a cellular automaton written into a neural network by hand (Ch. 7).',
    'The shortcut lesson: high accuracy that came from reading the rule off the first row, not from the behaviour (Ch. 8).',
    'An idea for future work, not a result of the thesis.' ] },
  { id: 'q8', q: 'Is the genotype filter really cheaper than a genetic algorithm?', ref: 'Ch. 6 §6.1.3', bullets: [
    'The genetic algorithm of Das et al. spends some 10⁷ to 10⁸ simulations on the search.',
    'The filter simulates nothing: a bitwise condition and 512 mean-field curves, in a fraction of a second.',
    'Verification costs both the same order: the saving is in the search.',
    'The evolved rules of Das et al. synchronise better, on lattices: φ⁹₂₃,₄₇ is not the best synchroniser available, and the rule spaces differ.' ] },
  { id: 'q9', q: 'How good is the mean-field approximation?', ref: 'Ch. 5 §5.3', bullets: [
    'It is one step deep and annealed: every round, contacts are redrawn at random.',
    'It ignores correlations and clustering, which the real network builds up at once.',
    'Hence simulation remains necessary; the genotype narrows the search, it does not replace it.' ] },
  { id: 'q10', q: 'Why not graph neural networks or graph kernels for classification?', ref: 'Ch. 9 §9.7', bullets: [
    'Beating them was not the aim; on social and chemical benchmarks they do better.',
    'The rule-based pipeline needs no node attributes, handles networks of any size, and comes with one interpretable parameter: the rule.' ] },
  { id: 'q11', q: 'Why odd resolutions and mirrored intervals?', ref: 'Ch. 5 §5.2, App. C', bullets: [
    'Complementation symmetry: swapping on and off must map the rule space onto itself.',
    'That needs the cut of the density axis mirrored around ½, with a middle interval that contains ½, hence an odd number of intervals.' ] },
  { id: 'q12', q: 'Why is the groundwater work not in the thesis?', ref: 'MACCA-GW', bullets: [
    'It is a spatially explicit cellular-automaton model of groundwater flow, not a network automaton.',
    'It does not fit the thesis’s story from rules to networks, so it is published separately, in the ACRI 2026 proceedings.' ] },
  { id: 'q13', q: 'Clamping is an intervention; what does merely swapping states do?', ref: 'Ch. 10 §10.3.1', bullets: [
    'Swap the most central person who is up with the least central one who is down, again and again.',
    'On preferential-attachment networks with 8 contacts: at most 10 swaps by closeness flip the outcome 90% of the time.',
    'Random swaps only push the outcome towards a coin toss: the 90% mark is never reached.' ] },
];
---
<Slide label="Backup slides" appendix id="backup" class="bk">
  <h2>Questions</h2>
  <ol class="index">
    {qs.map((x) => <li><a href={`#${x.id}`} data-go={x.id}>{x.q}</a></li>)}
  </ol>
</Slide>
{qs.map((x) => (
  <Slide label={x.q} appendix id={x.id} class="bk">
    <h2 class="bq">{x.q}</h2>
    <ul class="bl">{x.bullets.map((b) => <li>{b}</li>)}</ul>
    <p class="cite">{x.ref} · <a href="#backup" data-go="backup">back to the questions</a></p>
  </Slide>
))}

<style>
  .index { columns: 2; column-gap: calc(60 * var(--u)); font-size: calc(31 * var(--u)); display: block; margin-top: calc(10 * var(--u)); }
  .index li { break-inside: avoid; margin-bottom: calc(16 * var(--u)); }
  .index a, .cite a { color: var(--ink); text-decoration: none; }
  .index a:hover, .cite a:hover { color: var(--accent); }
  .bq { font-size: calc(56 * var(--u)); max-width: calc(1600 * var(--u)); }
  .bl { font-size: calc(38 * var(--u)); gap: calc(26 * var(--u)); max-width: calc(1600 * var(--u)); margin-top: calc(20 * var(--u)); }
</style>
